def find_minimum(arr):
    if not arr:
        raise ValueError("Array cannot be empty")

    min_element = arr[0]

    for element in arr[1:]:
        if element < min_element:
            min_element = element

    return min_element
```

This linear scan approach is actually optimal for an unsorted array—no clever divide-and-conquer or sorting trick beats O(n) here, since you fundamentally have to look at every element to guarantee you've found the true minimum. The common mistake people make is assuming there's a faster path somehow.

If you're dealing with a sorted array, of course, you'd just grab the first or last element in O(1) depending on ascending or descending order. But for the general case, this is it.

The algorithm initializes with the first element, then walks through the rest once, keeping the smallest value seen. Space complexity is O(1), which matters more than people think when arrays get large.
